<div style="font-family: Arial, sans-serif;">
    <h1 style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 20px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="text-align: center; font-style: italic;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Historical Sites and Tourism</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Take a Trip to the Panama Canal</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Reading: Comprehend descriptive texts about places or events. Reading: Summarize the main points of different types of texts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can comprehend descriptive texts about Indigenous events in Panama (e.g., "The festival has two main events: the traditional dance and the cooking contest. It's a time for the community to come together").
                Can summarize the main points of a text about Indigenous history (e.g., "Identify customs practiced at the Ngäbe-Buglé community." "Describe the beadwork used in Emberá traditional clothing." "List the types of dances performed during Guna Yala ceremonies.").
                Can comprehend from descriptive texts details about Indigenous arts and crafts (e.g., "The Guna people use bright colors like red, yellow, and blue in their art.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 15px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a large picture or short video (1 minute) of the Panama Canal, specifically showing ships transiting the locks and tourists at the Miraflores Visitors Center. The teacher asks students to brainstorm what they know about the place, using questions like: "What is this famous place?", "What does it connect?", "Why do people `visit` it?", "What can `visitors` `explore` there?". Students share their ideas in pairs and then with the class, activating prior knowledge and vocabulary from Lesson 1 such as `Panama Canal`, `historical sites`, `attract`, `visitor`, `explore`.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary from the brainstorm, such as `historical`, `cultural`, `monument`, `information`. The teacher specifically models the stress in multisyllabic words, for example, saying "CUL-ture" then "cul-TU-ral," "HIS-to-ry" then "his-TO-ri-cal." The teacher also models how to describe a place using present simple and simple past tenses, for instance: "The Panama Canal `is` a very important `historical site`." "It `was` a huge engineering project."</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of new or reactivated vocabulary and concepts.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is the Panama Canal a new place or an `ancient` one?" (Expected answer: "An `ancient`/`historical` one.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does a `monument` usually have a lot of `history` or no history?" (Expected answer: "It has a lot of `history`.")</li>
                    </ul></p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Historical Sites and Tourism".</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher provides students with a short informative article (approx. 150-180 words) titled "Exploring the Panama Canal: A Journey Through Time." The text uses present simple and simple past to `describe` the Canal's history and current status, integrating vocabulary such as `historical sites`, `landmarks`, `heritage`, `monument`, `destination`, `visitor`, `museum`, `tour`, `Causeway`, `transit`, `attract`, `explore`, `preserve`, `informative`, `renowned`. The text describes the Canal as a `renowned` engineering `monument` and `historical site` that `attracts` `visitors` from around the world. It mentions that tourists can `explore` the Miraflores Locks, `visit` the `museum` to `learn` about `Panama's heritage`, and `walk` along the `Causeway`.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reads and comprehends descriptive texts about historical landmarks and their significance.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the text individually.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> "What is the main idea of this article? Is it about shopping or about the `history` and importance of the Panama Canal?" (Students choose the best option).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students read specific statements and decide if they are True or False based on the text. For example: "The Panama Canal `is` an `ancient` ruin." (False) "Visitors `can explore` a `museum` at the Canal." (True)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students match descriptions (e.g., "The place for boats to cross") with `landmarks` mentioned in the text (e.g., "Miraflores Locks").</li>
                    </ul></p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension for descriptive texts, summarization of main points, use of present simple and simple past for descriptions, future "going to" for plans, possessive forms with 's, and target vocabulary.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Vocabulary & Grammar Cloze):</b> Students receive a worksheet with sentences from the reading text where key target vocabulary (e.g., `destination`, `heritage`, `preserve`, `attract`) and grammatical structures (e.g., present simple verbs, simple past verbs, possessive 's) are missing. Students fill in the blanks using words from a word bank. Example: "The Panama Canal ____ (`attracts`/`attracted`) many `visitors` each year." "It is a symbol of Panama's rich ____ (`heritage`)."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence Reordering & Summarization):</b> In pairs, students receive scrambled sentences related to a specific paragraph of the reading text (e.g., the historical section or the tourist attractions section). Their task is to reorder the sentences to form a coherent paragraph that `describes` that aspect of the Canal. They then read their reconstructed paragraph aloud, focusing on clear pronunciation and appropriate stress for words like `information` and `historical`. The teacher monitors and provides feedback on minimal pairs like /ɪ/ in "sit" (e.g., `history`) and /iː/ in "seat" (e.g., `visit`).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Question-Answering & Peer Check):</b> Students work in pairs. One student asks questions about the main points and details of the article (e.g., "What was the main purpose of the Panama Canal when it `was` built?", "What `are` two `landmarks` `visitors` `can explore` at the Canal?"). The other student answers based on their reading and comprehension. They then switch roles. Students use the text to justify their answers, aiming to `summarize` specific points. They practice understanding the intent and context of the descriptive information in the article.</li>
                    </ul></p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Historical Sites and Tourism".</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Short Tourist Fact Sheet Creation</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">In small groups (3-4 students), students will re-read the provided article and create a "Tourist Fact Sheet" (a short, bullet-point summary) for potential `visitors` interested in "Taking a Trip to the Panama Canal." The fact sheet must `describe` the Canal's main attractions, its `historical` importance, and what `visitors` `are going to` see or `explore`. They should use at least 5-7 of the target vocabulary words (e.g., `historical sites`, `landmarks`, `heritage`, `museum`, `Causeway`, `attract`, `explore`, `informative`, `renowned`) and demonstrate correct use of `present simple`, `simple past`, `going to` for plans, and `possessive 's` (e.g., "Panama's most famous landmark"). Students will focus on concisely `summarizing` the main points of the text, presenting the `information` in a `tourist-friendly` and `informative` way. This directly addresses the learning outcome of summarizing main points and comprehending details from descriptive texts about places and events.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy: Peer Assessment & Teacher Observation Checklist</b></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group briefly presents their "Tourist Fact Sheet" (reading it aloud) to another group. The receiving group provides feedback using a simple peer assessment checklist focused on clarity and completeness. The teacher circulates and uses an observation checklist to note group collaboration and individual student participation and accuracy in reading and summarizing.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and accuracy in summarizing the main points of the text.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of at least 5 target vocabulary words from the list (e.g., `historical sites`, `destination`, `attract`, `explore`, `heritage`).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct application of present simple, simple past, `going to` for plans, and possessive 's.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation, especially of multisyllabic words and minimal pairs focused on during the lesson.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and participation within the group.</li>
                    </ul></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick exit ticket with the following prompts:
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new fact I learned about the Panama Canal today is..."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The part of the lesson where I felt most confident `reading` and `summarizing` was..."</li>
                    </ul></p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about today's reading tasks, and how can I improve my reading comprehension for `descriptive texts`?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which `historical site` or `landmark` in Panama would I like to `explore` next, and why?"</li>
                    </ul></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b>
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did students successfully comprehend `descriptive texts` about the Panama Canal and `summarize` its main points? Were the comprehension tasks at the appropriate CEFR level?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What grammatical structures (e.g., `simple past`, `possessive 's`) or vocabulary items (e.g., `heritage`, `renowned`) proved challenging for students, and what adjustments or scaffolds are needed for subsequent lessons?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How effective was the peer assessment in promoting collaborative learning and accurate summarization?"</li>
                    </ul></p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The `information` and `vocabulary` learned today about the Panama Canal and other `historical sites` will be used in Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), where students `are going to` discuss travel plans and `describe` their favorite `historical sites` using the `informative` content they've acquired.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>